f 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129343 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129343 #edb/ref 1045 "Computing Areas Using a Change of Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129343 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129343 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129343 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129343 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e92aac25-8e3c-5b47-9748-65ca8bc64600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130083 #edb/ref 1061 26975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130083 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "32924515-5345-5bd8-8b6e-d8d617640311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130083 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130199 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130199 #edb/ref 1061 42405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130199 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5cd583ad-9fe0-5183-8354-e66c520bd7ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130199 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130200 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130200 #edb/ref 1061 42406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130200 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "51476dcf-0d6d-5530-9345-e9f13c6e0c72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130200 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130201 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130201 #edb/ref 1061 42417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130201 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5e3d0649-15dd-5adf-828b-27ae3bc49e74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130201 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130232 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130232 #edb/ref 1061 44616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130232 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f59c81ad-5b44-5523-9aeb-a14b98235004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130232 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130233 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130233 #edb/ref 1061 44617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130233 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4aa36004-91ee-5911-b639-83ff0faef28a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130233 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130454 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/8f/8f2d689080add393fad398d45d992e595996a12c3eca60c1c97e9a3e14720ed1.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant (shown above) bounded by the lines\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{3y-x}} = 3, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{3y-x}} = 6, \\qquad {\\color{red}{2x+y}}=4, \\qquad {\\color{red}{2x+y}}=6.\n$$\n\nThe area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows: \n\n![](images/11/11190d5d00f5f17be1349af2493735eb8dccbf9360c1ae4dfe771c04abf04179.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$  \n\nLooking at the lines that bound our region, we consider the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu = {\\color{blue}{3y-x}}, \\qquad v = {\\color{red}{2x+y}}\n$$\n\nNote that this change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/93/935c384a1c327786445c184e4a928e4d976f36a81c4e4edad23ef932e476b743.svg)\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=3y-x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3y-x=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\  & 3y-x=6 & ⟹\\, & u=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v = 2x+y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2x+y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4 \\\\  & 2x+y=6 & ⟹\\, & v=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq u \\leq 6, \\quad 4 \\leq v \\leq 6\\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/0d/0d55142f7713f3a9bfb93d1b1a383683af397a6edcd7c56a4338f25ab16b83a2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nOur transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\nu = 3y-x, \\qquad v = 2x+y.\n$$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac17.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{7}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (6-3)\\cdot (6-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130454 #edb/ref 1065 7037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130454 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0b502c14-a087-54fa-8a4d-3bda73b991ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130454 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130464 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a real interval $D = (a,b)\\subset \\mathbb R,$ the length of this interval can be represented by the integral\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int_a^b \\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nwhich we can also write as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int\\limits_D \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nSuppose we define an invertible function $u = u(x).$ By the change of variables formula, the length of our interval can also be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int\\limits_{\\Delta} \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Whenever we perform a change of variables, the domain of integration changes, too. This new integration domain is denoted $\\Delta.$  More technically, $\\Delta$ is the *image* of the $D$ under the action of $u(x).$\n\n- If we think of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}$ as a fraction, the $\\textrm d u$'s in our length formula appear to \"cancel out,\" leaving only the original $\\textrm d x.$ This makes the change of variables formula easy to remember.\n\nSo, to summarize,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int\\limits_D \\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\limits_{\\Delta} \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nSince the length of our interval is simply $b-a,$ it's doubtful that we'll ever use this formula in practice! However, it does indicate how we might use a change of variables to find the *area* of a complex region in two-dimensional space. Let's explore this idea in more detail." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130464 #edb/ref 1065 7682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130464 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "11fb8887-a8b0-514d-b23a-0cc6931ba17b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130464 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130465 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/6e/6ee5a19bf58db28574dc82aa3e6adcfd59015777bc503e66b9b9fd427278dcbf.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane shown above. The area of this region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nwhere we'll use the convention $\\textrm d A =  \\mathrm d x\\textrm d y$ when referring to integration with respect to the Cartesian coordinates $x$ and $y.$\n\nThis region looks pretty complicated. In such cases, it's often easier to find an invertible transformation $\\mathbf T$ (i.e., change of variables) whose inverse maps the region $D$ to simpler, preferably rectangular, region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane. \n\n![](images/66/668b8a0dae060c7929b92c6abe3601b0ede0b8444d92f33f4a8b5627410a4447.png)\n\n \n\nSo, rather than computing the required area by integrating with respect to $x$ and $y,$ we can instead perform the transformation and integrate with respect to $u$ and $v.$ \n\nTo carry out this process, we apply the **change of variables formula**\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$ \n\nNotice that this formula is analogous to the one we saw earlier for computing the length of an interval. In particular:\n\n- If we consider the Jacobian as a kind of fraction, the $\\partial(u,v)$ and $\\textrm d u\\textrm d v$ parts appear to \"cancel out,\" similar to the previous formula.\n\n- Note that $\\mathbf T$ could be orientation-reversing. We take the absolute value of the Jacobian to ensure that $D$ and $\\Delta$ have the same orientation.\n\nFinally, note that the change of variables formula assumes that $\\mathbf T$ is a $C^1$ transformation and has no critical points in the interior of $\\Delta.$\n\nOften, the most challenging part is finding a suitable change of variables. The main idea is to get a nice rectangular shape with sides parallel to the axes in the $uv$-plane. In other words, we require a change of variables that reduces the equations of our curves to something like the following:\n\n$$\nu = \\textrm{const}_1, \\quad\nu = \\textrm{const}_2, \\quad\nv = \\textrm{const}_3, \\quad\nv = \\textrm{const}_4\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's look at a concrete example of computing an area using the change of variables formula." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130465 #edb/ref 1065 7683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130465 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d5fba407-4d6f-55e7-a93a-4f1e6fe6369f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130465 #edb/ref 1067 "The Change of Variables Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371886 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371886 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160857" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371886 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371886 #edb/ref 1071 "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n3y=4x+1,\\,3y=4x-2,\\,3x=2y+1,\\,3x=2y+3\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371886 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1533ae23-2a8c-5746-8cc6-278953144598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371886 #edb/ref 1074 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n4x-3y=-1,\\,4x-3y=2,\\,3x-2y=1,\\,3x-2y=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=4x-3y,\\,v=3x-2y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=4x-3y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 4x-3y=-1\\,⟹\\,u=-1 \\\\  & 4x-3y=2\\,⟹\\,u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=3x-2y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3x-2y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & 3x-2y=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3}\n$$\n\n![](images/5d/5dc4be989300dca3e52b7ff1dfb6a21c60409976fe55d42f5ae530f033725a64.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-8+9 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(2-(-1))\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371887 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371887 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371887 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371887 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371887 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371887 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371887 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371887 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371887 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f732fda0-f223-5e8e-9cd7-3bad366f7805" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371888 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371888 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371888 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ccecda64-e614-576a-95ef-e05124675fbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371889 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371889 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371889 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c3dbc0ee-bd56-5d09-82ae-0a50d663a617" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371890 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371890 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371890 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7e1c272c-a26e-56c4-b420-6c0e9237be97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371891 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371891 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371891 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ad9488a7-26bc-5807-abd0-f5550f336827" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371892 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371892 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371892 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "49a9bf82-6b9b-5af5-bd83-8f2fc3e179b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371913 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371913 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160891" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371913 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371913 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{xy},\\,v={x}^{2}y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2x},\\,y=\\frac{1}{3x},\\,y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371913 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75940fb8-f294-5aab-8f7b-cfe4e75211a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371913 #edb/ref 1074 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{xy},\\,v={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{1}{xy}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{2x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3}\n$$\n\n![](images/05/05d2ca47db245b147cb52f95a474a60ece144931c8b6e1e40c6628a99b53d690.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y} & -\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2xy & \\text{ }\\text{ }{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=y=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}=\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371914 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371914 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371914 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371914 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371914 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371914 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371914 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371914 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371914 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "49ac9b17-c604-511f-9b48-3b87989affec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371915 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371915 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371915 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14d59625-eac9-589e-ab14-341551eea08f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371916 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371916 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371916 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7b889d37-5d45-5818-9d27-40379a4533ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371917 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371917 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371917 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b06248ff-2151-5554-b5af-ab842ba5705a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371918 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371918 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371918 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a1e1a6f5-fc15-5138-88ce-fdee68bb88d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371919 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371919 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371919 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5b52d6ac-ffd9-5f34-ba9e-30bc4a86d030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373339 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373339 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373339 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373339 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y+3x,\\,v=x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(y+3x)^{2}-x=3$ and the line $x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373339 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "672b45e6-0aa1-59c9-9e64-dd97d59af187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373339 #edb/ref 1074 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+3x,\\,v=x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $y+3x=u$ and $x=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3x)^{2}-x=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=3\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-3 \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 2 \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,{u}^{2}-3\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\n![](images/26/2687f0cc14eb0b476ea5f4ba7272e7f248bb7dcb276a0e2b0c39e551501b011d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(-1)^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}-3}^{1}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[v]_{{u}^{2}-3}^{1}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(1-({u}^{2}-3))\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[4u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =16-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218637334